  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nNorth of a warm front across southeast Kansas, our area remains cool \nand unsettled this afternoon. Scattered showers and weak \nthunderstorms are still ongoing around the Topeka area, but are \ngradually fading away as isentropic ascent weakens. While \ntemperatures should approach 80 towards central KS where more \nsunshine has occurred, expecting highs to hold in the 70s across the \nmajority of the area given the persistent cloud cover. Overnight, \nthe warm front should begin to move north across our area. Expecting\nanother round of showers and thunderstorms to develop as this \noccurs, mainly north of I-70. Similar to this morning's storms, some \nsmall hail could occur with these, but limited elevated instability \nshould keep the severe weather threat low. High PWAT and favorable \nhodographs for slower-moving efficient rain-producers may result in \nlocally heavy rain of 2-4\". However the main area of rain should \nshift north into Nebraska by mid morning as the front continues \nnorth, so the overall flood threat seems low.\n\nWith the warm front fully north of the area, Monday will be a warm \nday across the entire area. Highs warm into the 90s, and with \ndewpoints near 70 heat indices will approach 100 for most. \nMeanwhile, an upper low over the Northern Plains will be pushing a \ncold front into far north-central KS. Convergence along the front \nshould allow for some scattered storms to develop over the late \nafternoon and evening hours. Moderate shear and instability appear \nfavorable for some northeastward moving clusters capable of \nproducing large hail and damaging winds. \n\nWhile the severe potential should stay confined to far north-central \nKS Monday night, a similar setup appears likely farther east on \nTuesday as the front slowly pushes farther through the area. Still \nsome questions on the exact eastward extent, but most guidance takes \nthe front somewhere near Topeka by mid-day. In the sunnier skies \nahead of the front, highs will approach 90 across far east-central \nKS. Any storm that develops along the boundary will see steeper low-\nlevel lapse rates and better instability, favorable for some \nisolated damaging winds or small hail. Meanwhile behind the front, \nsteady CAA and thicker cloud cover amidst rounds of elevated storms \nshould keep temperatures in the 70s and even 60s. Given multiple \nrounds of rain and storms through early Wednesday, there is \npotential for a widespread several inches of rain, particularly \nacross portions of northern KS near the Nebraska border. \nDeterministic guidance hints at smaller pockets of 3-6\", so we'll \nhave to watch flooding potential if multiple rounds travel over the \nsame areas.\n\nLater in the week into next weekend, uncertainty increases. The \nfrontal boundary should lift back north a bit, but remain generally \nclose by. So we'll see a bit of a warming trend, but continue to see \na decent range in temperatures across the forecast area. Rain \nchances continue along the front, as several weak upper \nperturbations move across the central Plains. Difficult to pin these \nsmall waves down at this range, so keeping broad PoPs in place \nthrough Saturday for now.",
